A||N||4||JOHN R. ALLEN III||Zero to 60 in a micro-second||||1||
Z||000000||JOHN R. ALLEN III||03-05-2001||09:57 AM||||I just installed white face gauge overlays on my 1986 fiero speedometer, etc.  To do this I carefully remove the needle indicators and pre-painted the stock gauges with white spray paint.  The speedometer needle was the toughest to remove, but it did not seem to disturb anything.  The problem is that the speedometer does not work at all up to around 40 mph or so and then jumps and locks on 60 mph.  Once you return to stop the gauge will generally return to zero only to repeat the same 60 mph jump when you accelerate again.  Does anyone have a clue?  Could prying the needle off (very carfully I add) damaged something?  Could slight overspray gotten in to something even though the tack got the same treatment and worked fine.||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroJoe||03-05-2001||10:24 AM||||Sounds like sticking could be a possibility, but also check to make sure that you have reconnected all the IP electrical connections tightly.  I didnt spray mine white before I put the stickers on, for the fear of ruining something.  They look pretty good though, for not having the black painted before hand.  I guess just try scraping some paint away from where the needle is, I wouldnt use any solvents at all.  Just maybe the tip of a knife or something to scrape away anything that got in there, and then blow it away.<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||JOHN R. ALLEN III||03-05-2001||10:53 AM||||Thanks for the reply.  I checked and rechecked connections, sticking "chads" etc.  No problems there.||||reg||1
Z||000003||beer_stud_21||03-05-2001||02:36 PM||||You gotta watch out for those chads... They will screw you over big time if your not carefull.....<P>-M||||reg||1
